  "query_preview": "..A concerning situation will evolve across Arizona \nover the next two days, with threats of flooding/flash flooding, \nsevere thunderstorms, and tornadoes. A strong low pressure system \noff the California coast is beginning to move inland. This is well\npositioned to carry a deep moisture surge of tropical and \nsubtropical origins, as well as provide an ideal environment for \nsevere weather. The low eventually lifts through the Great Basin \non Thursday, dragging a cold front through the region late \nThursday into Friday.\n\nThis morning...The first surge of tropical moisture has arrived, and \nactivity is ramping up quickly early this morning. Current \nmesoanalysis shows a 50-55kt jet overhead at 500mb, as well as a \nstrengthening southeasterly low-level jet advecting warm air and \nhigh dewpoints into northern AZ. This has allowed for increasing \ninstability south of the Rim during the overnight hours, with MLCAPE \nvalues currently around 250-500 J/kg in Yavapai and Gila counties. \nCoupled with bulk shear around 50kts and storm relative helicity 100-\n200 m2/s2, we are seeing scattered discrete supercell thunderstorms \ninitiating along the terrain as of 2 AM MST. This is expected to \ndecline in extent over the next few hours as conditions become \nslightly more unfavorable, however continued thunderstorms activity \nis expected especially further west. Severe thunderstorms bringing 1-\n2 inch hail have remained the primary threat thus far. A secondary \nthreat of flash flooding is also in play this morning given the \narrival of deep moisture. However fast storm motion and scattered \ncoverage will allow rain accumulations to likely remain below flash \nflooding concerns outside of sensitive burn scars.\n\nToday through Thursday evening...Threats of flash flooding and \nsevere thunderstorms continue through the period. For flooding, \nwe will remain in a continuous fetch of deep tropical/subtropical \nmoisture with modest instability and enhanced low-level flow. \nPrecipitable Water (a measure of atmospheric moisture) will rise \nto 0.8-1.2 inches across most of our area. This is highly \nanomalous for mid-October. Despite the fast expected storm \nmotion, periods of training or back-building storms are possible \nand could lead to locally heavy rainfall rates with flash flooding\nconcerns. Also several rounds of heavy rain are expected for \nmost, and longer duration flooding of rivers, streams, normally \ndry washes, drainage basins, etc is possible. This is exacerbated\nfurther by very saturated surface conditions for all of northern \nAZ. As a result, a Flood Watch will be in effect beginning at 5 AM\nMST today and continuing through 11 PM MST Thursday. The threat \nof flooding ends on Thursday evening as a cold front sweeps from \nwest to east and brings much drier air in its wake.\n\nFor the severe threat, an unstable environment with an impressive \nshear profile will be maintained through the period. The jet at \n500mb will continue to strengthen on Wednesday as the low pressure \nsystem slowly approaches. Despite cloud cover to limit \ndestabilization, hi-res guidance remains adamant that CAPE values\nup to 1000-1500 J/kg will develop by Wednesday afternoon with \nhelp from low-level moisture advection and steepening mid-level \nlapse rates. Several rounds of severe weather are possible, with \nthe peak threat window beginning late Wednesday afternoon through \nevening. Stronger supercell storms will be capable of large 2+ \ninch hail, damaging wind gusts, and even a few tornadoes. SPC \nmaintains an Enhanced risk (level 3 out of 5) of severe weather \ncentered along the Mogollon Rim on Wednesday. Into Thursday, the \nsevere threat looks to shift a bit further east towards the \nEastern Mogollon Rim and White Mountains with potential for strong\nstorms along the propagating cold front. \n\nBe prepared and have a way to receive warnings!\n\nFriday and onward...Activity is expected to trend downward by Friday \ninto the weekend after the main trough axis pushes past Northern AZ \nand drier air moves in. A closed low may linger across western AZ, \nand slight chances for showers and thunderstorms remain in the \nforecast each day. Expect lighter winds and temperatures remaining \nbelow normal otherwise.",
